     There isn't any such command.  This is a hallmark of the
roguelike
genre.  Once you die, that particular game is over (although nethack
has
an item called amulet of life-saving; wearing one lets you survive
most
types of death, but the item gets used up in the process).

     If you play in explore mode (or in debug mode, which is also
known
as wizard mode), you get prompted when killed and can choose not to
die.
Also, when you restore the save file for an explore mode or debug mode
game, you'll be asked whether to keep it (instead of automatically
discarding it as happens in normal mode), making it feasible to quit
the current circumstance and re-restore at the previous starting
point.
That's intended for experimentation and testing (and debugging), not
for extended use once you've learned how to play.  Overall, the threat
of "permanent death" tends to make game play be more interesting.

     The next question after "can I save without exiting?" might be
"can I copy the save file before restoring it, then put it back in
place after the game has deleted it?".  Answer is "it depends"; for
single-user systems like Windows you could, for a typical multi-user
Unix set up you usually can't unless you're the sysadmin.  Such file
manipulation is commonly known as "save scumming" and is likely to
trigger a flame war here when anyone advocates doing it.  Explore
mode provides the ability to achieve this without going behind the
program's back, at a trivial cost of not putting your final score
into "record", nethack's scoreboard file.


